position: 0,0
---
<<set $playerName = "Robin">>

// Everything after a double slash is a comment, in the header or the body.
This lesson is about statements. A variable is injected into text with a $-sign, like your name: $playerName.

[[Show me escaping.|Escaping]]
===
title: Escaping
tags:
speaker: Martin McOwl
colorId: 0
position: 320,0
---
To print a literal dollar sign you escape it: \$5. A literal backslash is written \\. Any character can be escaped this way, so a link keeps both its slashes: https:\//www.dialoguebranch.com

[[What about markup?|Markup]]
===
title: Markup
tags:
speaker: Martin McOwl
colorID: 0
note: this node keeps the legacy colorID spelling and a custom tag on purpose
position: 640,0
---
Dialogue Branch does not interpret markup. Tags such as <b>bold</b> are passed straight through to your client, untouched.

[[That is all.|End]]
